<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panama Papers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2016">2016</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> Papers were 11.5 million leaked documents from the Panamanian law firm Mossack Fonseca, published from April 2016 by the International Consortium of Investigative Journalists and partner newspapers. They revealed how politicians, officials and wealthy individuals around the world used offshore shell companies to hide assets and avoid tax. The leak forced the resignation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iceland">Iceland</a>'s prime minister and damaged Panama's reputation as a financial centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/panama-papers.jpg" alt="Panama Papers" width="320" height="165" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JayCoop · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Countries_implicated_in_the_Panama_Papers.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Mossack Fonseca was a Panamanian law firm and corporate service provider whose services included incorporating and operating shell companies in friendly jurisdictions on behalf of clients. It managed more than 300,000 companies over the years, with the number of active companies peaking at more than 80,000 in 2009, and its clients came from more than 100 countries. It worked with more than 14,000 banks, law firms and other intermediaries. Owning an offshore company is generally legal, and the reasons for it can be lawful, such as estate planning or holding funds in dollars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>An anonymous source calling itself John Doe leaked the documents to the journalist Bastian Obermayer of the German newspaper Süddeutsche Zeitung. The paper asked the International Consortium of Investigative Journalists for help in analysing 2.6 terabytes of data. Journalists from 107 media organisations in 80 countries worked on them for more than a year, and the first stories appeared on 3 April 2016 together with 150 of the documents. The source insisted on encrypted communication and never met the reporters. In a statement released on 6 May, the source cited income inequality as the reason for the leak.</p><p>The firm told its clients on 1 April that its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/email">email</a> had been hacked. Security experts noted that it had not encrypted its emails and appeared to be running a three-year-old version of the Drupal web software with known vulnerabilities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The leak covered 214,488 offshore entities, over 210,000 of them in twenty-one jurisdictions, more than half in the British Virgin Islands. Reporters found that some of the shell companies seemed to have been used for fraud, tax evasion and evading international <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a>; more than 30 clients were at some point blacklisted by the US Treasury. Initial reports named five then-heads of state or government, and officials and associates of leaders in more than forty other countries. Mossack Fonseca said that nothing in the documents suggested it had done anything illegal.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama">Panama</a> objected to the name Panama Papers, fearing damage to the country's image. Its attorney general announced an investigation on 4 April, and on 12 April prosecutors searched the firm's offices for 27 hours. President Varela announced a commission led by the Nobel laureate Joseph Stiglitz. The ICIJ's director, Gerard Ryle, called the leak probably the biggest blow the offshore world had ever taken.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Panama Papers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…ship registration from 1925 eventually made Panama’s merchant fleet the largest in the world by tonnage, the Colón Free Zone was created in 1948, and a banking law of 1970 turned Panama City into an offshore financial centre, whose secrecy was exposed by the <b>Panama Papers</b> of 2016.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-panama">Republic of Panama · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
